#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055535} :activity/id #uuid "a56c5450-2be5-51a0-b60f-0dfe05b153cc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050218 :topic/id #uuid "370d229e-4b9a-5ee8-b857-c25ac383bf2c" :topic/math-academy-id 32} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055965 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves of the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{i}^{1} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{3} &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{4} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the fourth power, we return to the value of $1$, which can also be written as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^0 = 1\n$$\n\nThis means that if we continue to increase the powers, we would cycle through the same four values once again, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3f3a42dd591daf109e474eec99e446706e0d8e46f1252540839cd50d957463.png)\n\nSo, for $\\textrm{i}^5$ through $\\textrm{i}^8$, the exact same pattern repeats. Remembering that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^4 = 1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{i}^5&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^6&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1 \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^7&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^8&=\\textrm{i}^4 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $\\textrm{i}^9$ through to $\\textrm{i}^{12}$, the pattern repeats again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{i}^9&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{10}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^2 &=-1 \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{11}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^3 &= -\\textrm{i} \\\\[5pt] \\textrm{i}^{12}&=(\\textrm{i}^4)^2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^4 &= 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to simplify a higher integer power of $\\textrm{i}$, we *divide the power by $4$ and raise $\\textrm{i}$ to the remainder*.\n\nLet's see this idea in action." :tutorial/id #uuid "be735724-c1e7-5d1c-b396-61d58ee81903" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2762 :tutorial/title "The Cyclic Nature of Powers of i"} :step/id #uuid "d1c6ab98-c462-5fc7-9ff5-b61c7e1953a7" :step/math-academy-id 13775 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054614 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054525 :question/id #uuid "2777aa24-9055-55d3-9548-3aef0586bea7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3942"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e8ad55-058a-5d71-ac05-3f8533f8bd46" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048428}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234195}] :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Small Positive Integer Power"} :step/id #uuid "0f619483-42e5-52ac-a546-41e48b85f75c" :step/math-academy-id 13779 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054526 :question/id #uuid "71af77a8-8b16-59f5-b6fb-0c344c3b9f90" :question/math-academy-id "e-3943"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "760a1a5b-4c06-547d-9248-a6acb97c4b84" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234476}] :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Positive Integer Power Using the Cyclic Property"} :step/id #uuid "7a034e07-bee1-5908-a898-6d007aa44066" :step/math-academy-id 13780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055536}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055535 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055966 :tutorial/content "Let's compute the first few powers of $\\textrm{i}$, where\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i} = \\sqrt{-1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\textrm{i}^{0} &= 1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{1} &= \\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{2} &= -1 \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{3} &= \\textrm{i}^{2} \\cdot \\textrm{i} = (-1) \\cdot \\textrm{i} =-\\textrm{i} \\\\\n\\textrm{i}^{4} &= \\textrm{i}^{2} \\cdot \\textrm{i}^{2} = (-1) \\cdot (-1) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use these results to evaluate expressions containing powers of $\\textrm{i}$. Let's take a look at some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "6fe2451a-f8be-5178-a84f-66817536929d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2763 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8a8a7097-d5fe-52e6-b191-a54ff1ff6ee1" :step/math-academy-id 13784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054616 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054527 :question/id #uuid "3bc3d3e9-6745-5021-b571-a301851d7bb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3944"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c51033-ad39-5776-9829-cf573214ef03" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050697}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234152}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Involving Powers of i Using the Laws of Exponents"} :step/id #uuid "1d9fa15b-505e-5efb-acca-ed49130fabb6" :step/math-academy-id 13785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055537}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055537 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054617 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054528 :question/id #uuid "a1b59f3b-4378-5b1e-b21e-a865369e6e01" :question/math-academy-id "e-3964"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa5128ae-312f-5a12-9a19-9158fa7ddb9a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050495}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234213}] :knowledge-point/title "Raising i to a Negative Power"} :step/id #uuid "5aabf84e-eb5f-5695-bef0-ba3fb10691b5" :step/math-academy-id 13932}] :activity/title "The Cyclic Property of the Imaginary Unit" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054894}]}
